export class EmailProviderError extends Error {
  readonly status: number;
  readonly retryAfter: string | null;
  constructor(status: number, retryAfter: string | null) {
    super(`email provider answered ${status}`);
    this.status = status;
    this.retryAfter = retryAfter;
  }
}

export async function sendEmail(baseUrl: string, apiKey: string, message: object, signal?: AbortSignal) {
  const timeout = AbortSignal.timeout(2_000);
  const res = await fetch(`${baseUrl}/v1/messages`, {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json", Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}` },
    body: JSON.stringify(message),
    signal: signal ? AbortSignal.any([signal, timeout]) : timeout,
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new EmailProviderError(res.status, res.headers.get("Retry-After"));
  return res.json();
}

import http from "node:http";
import type { AddressInfo } from "node:net";

// A fake email provider that takes 5 seconds to answer.
const slow = http.createServer((_req, res) => {
  setTimeout(() => res.end("{}"), 5_000);
});
slow.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", async () => {
  const { port } = slow.address() as AddressInfo;
  const started = performance.now();
  try {
    await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/v1/messages`, {
      method: "POST",
      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(2_000),
    });
  } catch (err) {
    const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - started);
    console.log(`${(err as Error).name} after ${ms} ms: ${(err as Error).message}`);
  }
  slow.closeAllConnections();
  slow.close();
});

steps.body:
Many failures don't last: a dropped connection, an instance restarting, a 503 from a server that's briefly overloaded. Trying again a moment later usually works. But retries done carelessly turn a small problem into a big one, and a dependency that's really down needs the opposite of a retry: to be left alone for a while.

## What to retry

Retry only what can succeed next time, and only what's safe to repeat:

- **Retry** timeouts, refused or reset connections, `429 Too Many Requests` (after its `Retry-After`), and `502`, `503`, and `504`, which mean a server was down or overloaded.
- **Don't retry** other 4xx codes. A 400, 401, 404, 409, or 422 gets the same answer every time.
- **Only repeat what's safe to repeat.** GET, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent, as the web course explained, so a second copy changes nothing. A POST that timed out may have worked, so retrying it can book twice, unless it carries an idempotency key, which is the next part.

## Backoff and jitter

Retrying at once mostly hits the same overloaded server again. **Exponential backoff** waits longer after each failure: 200 milliseconds, then 400, then 800, doubling up to a ceiling. And when one server blips, hundreds of clients fail at the same moment, so even with backoff they retry together, in waves that knock it over again. **Jitter** adds randomness: with **full jitter**, each wait is a random time between zero and the backoff, which spreads the retries out.

```diagram reliability-retry-jitter
Without jitter, clients retry in waves. With full jitter, the same retries spread out.
```

Three limits keep retries honest. Give up after a few **attempts**, three to five. Stop when the request's **deadline** runs out. And retry at **one layer only**: if the app, its client library, and a proxy each try 3 times, one failure becomes 27 calls. Some teams also keep a **retry budget**, retries at most 10% of all calls, so a real outage doesn't double the load.

Node has no retry helper built in, and many teams write a short one like this, `src/lib/retry.ts`, a general version of the `withRetry` from the data integrity lesson, which retried only serialization failures:

```ts
import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

type RetryOptions = {
  attempts: number;
  baseMs: number;
  maxMs: number;
  retryable: (err: unknown) => boolean;
  onRetry?: (err: unknown, attempt: number, waitMs: number) => void;
};

export async function retry<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, opts: RetryOptions): Promise<T> {
  for (let attempt = 1; ; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt >= opts.attempts || !opts.retryable(err)) throw err;
      // Full jitter: a random wait between 0 and the exponential backoff.
      const backoff = Math.min(opts.maxMs, opts.baseMs * 2 ** (attempt - 1));
      const waitMs = Math.round(Math.random() * backoff);
      opts.onRetry?.(err, attempt, waitMs);
      await sleep(waitMs);
    }
  }
}
```

## Circuit breakers

When a dependency is really down, every call waits for its timeout and then fails, retries add more calls, and the dependency can't recover under the load. A **circuit breaker** notices, and stops calling it for a while, like the breaker in a fuse box that cuts the power before the wires burn.

```diagram reliability-breaker-states
A breaker opens on failures, waits, then lets a trial call decide.
```

It has three states. **Closed** is normal: calls go through, and the breaker counts failures. When too many fail, say half of the last 20, it trips **open**: for a cool-down, maybe 30 seconds, every call fails at once without trying. Then it goes **half-open** and lets a few trial calls through. If they work, it closes, and if they fail, it opens again.

Failing fast is only half of it. The other half is the **fallback**, what to do instead. Roombook puts a breaker on its Redis cache reads: while Redis is down, the breaker is open, and reads go straight to PostgreSQL without waiting half a second each for a timeout. In Node, **opossum** is the standard breaker. Wrap the function, give it the thresholds and a fallback, and call `fire` instead of the function:

```ts sketch
const cacheRead = new CircuitBreaker((key: string) => redis.get(key), {
  timeout: 500,
  errorThresholdPercentage: 50,
  volumeThreshold: 10,
  resetTimeout: 30_000,
});
cacheRead.fallback(() => null); // open: treat it as a miss
cacheRead.on("open", () => console.warn("redis breaker open: reading from PostgreSQL"));

const cached = await cacheRead.fire(key);
```

A **bulkhead** is the breaker's partner, named after the walls that keep one flooded compartment from sinking a ship. It caps how many calls to one dependency can be in flight, say 10 to the email provider, so a slow dependency can only take 10 of your connections, never all of them. In Node, a small counter or a library like `p-limit` caps calls in flight.

